The migrated file is at `out/module.ts`. It type-checks with the project's tsconfig; the only errors are in the original `src/module.ts`, because lodash isn't installed.

I couldn't run the original, so I couldn't compare outputs directly. Instead I checked each function's behaviour against lodash's semantics.

```ts
import { keyBy } from 'es-toolkit';
import { kebabCase, toNumber } from 'es-toolkit/compat';
// Row, Config, and the three exported functions keep their signatures
// toSlug -> kebabCase(label); byNameKey -> keyBy(rows, (row) => row.name); parseAmount -> toNumber(text)
```

- **`toSlug`**: I used `kebabCase` from `es-toolkit/compat`, not the core one. In a quick test the core version splits ordinals ("a 1st" becomes "a-1-st"), while lodash and the compat version keep them ("a-1st"). Your input range allows digits between letters, so the core version would change results.
- **`byNameKey`**: core `keyBy` takes a function rather than lodash's `'name'` string shorthand, so I added a small arrow function, `(row) => row.name`.
- **`parseAmount`**: only `es-toolkit/compat` has `toNumber`, and it follows lodash's rules.
- **Lodash**: no lodash import remains.

**Size**: `npm run size -- out/module.ts` reports 1735 bytes (min+gzip), with 91 bytes of my own code. The first attempt, using core `kebabCase`, was 346 bytes with 88 bytes of own code. I didn't keep it because it changes ordinal results. I couldn't get a baseline for the original file because the size script fails without lodash installed.

